{"authored" [{"question" "q-346806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\frac{x+3}{(x+2)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{x+3}{(x+2)^{2}}=$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x+2$ |  | $(x+2)^{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce6fd9130710f6f82288974366e6ed398a205420c8d0ca28f3d611f8be15c012" "basis" 615 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above equality by $(x+2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\nx+3=A(x+2)+B.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+3 & =A(-2+2)+B \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+3 & =A(0+2)+1 \\\\ 3 & =2A+1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce6fd9130710f6f82288974366e6ed398a205420c8d0ca28f3d611f8be15c012" "basis" 615 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346806" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66b1124bc06eaefc8dee48c0246afb3d6799036270ec7eb9de3f06955de9e220" "basis" 615 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346806" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce6fd9130710f6f82288974366e6ed398a205420c8d0ca28f3d611f8be15c012" "basis" 615 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-346806" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce6fd9130710f6f82288974366e6ed398a205420c8d0ca28f3d611f8be15c012" "basis" 615 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+17x+8}{x(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+2}+\\frac{C}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{D}{(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ are real constants, determine the value of $AB+CD.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce6fd9130710f6f82288974366e6ed398a205420c8d0ca28f3d611f8be15c012" "basis" 615 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $x(x+2)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+17x+8=A(x+2)^{3}+Bx(x+2)^{2}+Cx(x+2)+Dx.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{3}+8(-2)^{2}+17(-2)+8 & =A(-2+2)^{3}+B(-2)(-2+2)^{2}+C(-2)(-2+2)+D(-2) \\\\ -8+32-34+8 & =-2D \\\\ -8+32-34+8 & =-2D \\\\ -2 & =-2D \\\\ D & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{3}+8(0)^{2}+17(0)+8 & =A(0+2)^{3}+B(0)(0+2)^{2}+C(0)(0+2)+D(0) \\\\ 8 & =8A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-1$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}+8(-1)^{2}+17(-1)+8 & =1(-1+2)^{3}+B(-1)(-1+2)^{2}+C(-1)(-1+2)+1(-1) \\\\ -1+8-17+8 & =1-B-C-1 \\\\ B & =2-C.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=-3$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{3}+8(-3)^{2}+17(-3)+8 & =1(-3+2)^{3}+B(-3)(-3+2)^{2}+C(-3)(-3+2)+1(-3) \\\\ -27+72-51+8 & =-1-3B+3C-3 \\\\ 3B & =3C-6 \\\\ B & =C-2.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-C & =C-2 \\\\ -2C & =-4 \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $C$ into $(1)$  to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\nB=2-2=0\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nAB+CD=1(0)+2(1)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce6fd9130710f6f82288974366e6ed398a205420c8d0ca28f3d611f8be15c012" "basis" 615 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "66b1124bc06eaefc8dee48c0246afb3d6799036270ec7eb9de3f06955de9e220" "basis" 615 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953040/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-cap